{"id":1458,"date":"2020-11-27T19:57:32","date_gmt":"2020-11-28T03:57:32","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1458"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:38","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:38","slug":"rokit-states","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/rokit-states\/","title":{"rendered":"States"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162604f4c\" name=\"6a22162604f4c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How to control state<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you remember how to write if statements or variables, you&#8217;ll be able to create states for the Trailer Bot! For this bot, you&#8217;ll need to create states so it can work with a remote control, with a program, and go fast.<\/p>\n<p>The first thing you need to do is create a variable named state so you can keep track of the mode you&#8217;re in. However, variables can only hold letters and numbers, so you&#8217;ll need to assign each mode a different value. We assigned 0 as fast, autonomous as 1, and remote control as 3.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, create if and else if statements so the program knows which state the Trailer Bot is in.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int state = 0;\r\n \r\nif (state == 0)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for fast mode\r\n}\r\nelse if (state == 1)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for autonomous mode\r\n}\r\nelse if (state == 2)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for remote control mode\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216260508d\" name=\"6a2216260508d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Label each state<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]It&#8217;s easy to get your states and the numbers that go with them mixed up, especially if you start adding more states. To make your life easier, create variables before your if statements so you can keep track of everything. If you do this, you just need to remember the name of the variable when you need it in your program!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int fast = 0;\r\nint auton = 1;\r\nint remote = 2;\r\nint state = auton;\r\n \r\nif (state == fast)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for fast mode\r\n}\r\nelse if (state == auton)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for autonomous mode\r\n}\r\nelse if (state == remote)\r\n{\r\n  \/\/ code for remote control mode\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221626050f8\" name=\"6a221626050f8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This is an example code using states.\u00a0When this code runs, it will print out a different message on the serial monitor and then wait a certain amount of time depending on the state it&#8217;s in.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">\/\/ state values\r\nint slow = 0;\r\nint medium = 1;\r\nint fast = 2;\r\nint state = fast;\r\n \r\nvoid setup()\r\n{\r\n  Serial.begin(9600); \/\/ start Serial monitor\r\n}\r\n \r\nvoid loop()\r\n{\r\n if (state == fast) \/\/ fast state\r\n {\r\n   Serial.println(\"state is fast\");\r\n   delay(100); \/\/ short delay\r\n }\r\n else if (state == medium) \/\/ medium state\r\n {\r\n   Serial.println(\"state is medium\");\r\n   delay(250); \/\/ medium delay\r\n }\r\n else if (state == slow) \/\/ slow state\r\n {\r\n   Serial.println(\"state is slow\");\r\n   delay(500); \/\/ long delay\r\n }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22162605142\" name=\"6a22162605142\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Video<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Still not sure about states? Watch the video![\/vc_column_text][vc_video link=&#8221;https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/watch?v=ow-OUJbKiJo&#8221; align=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 5 How to control state [vc_column_text]If you remember how to write if statements or variables, you'll be able to create states for the Trailer Bot! For this bot,&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/rokit-states\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1736,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1760,1777,1808,1793,626,1533,1438,144,63,209],"lesson-tag":[2163,2122,2171,2190],"class_list":["post-1458","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","lesson-tag-trailer","module-trailer","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1458","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1458\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1459,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1458\/revisions\/1459"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1736"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive